  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showery and stormy pattern continues through\nSunday. \n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms had erupted this afternoon \nin response to several modes of forcing for ascent, with the \nstrongest storms in the VA Foothills and Piedmont. Much of this \nactivity has started to wane, and coverage should continue to \ndecrease over the next couple of hours. A band of mid level \nvorticity was located from Maryland to southern OH to MO, ending\nwith a significant short wave also located near MO. This wave \nis progged to move east and deepen the overall short wave trough\ntonight through Sunday as it does so. At the surface, a cold \nfront was located from coastal VA and snaked through our \nforecast area in VA before stretching towards the ArkLaTex. \n\nFor tomorrow, the front will move back northeast as a warm \nfront, bringing moderate rain and some thunderstorms to the \nentire area before it exits to the east after midnight Saturday \nnight. Storms are not expected to become severe at this time. \nHowever, isolated damaging wind gusts will be possible with the \nstrongest storms. In addition, urban, small stream, and isolated\nflash flooding may be possible as convection trains along the \nfront and with repeated rounds expected. \n\nA few showers/storms may develop Sunday afternoon as another\ncold front moves through, but not expecting anything strong or\norganized. This would mainly impact the Blue Ridge eastward. \nBehind this front, much drier and cooler air arrives for the \nbeginning of next week, perhaps lasting through Wednesday as a \nlarge upper ridge builds in. By Thursday, we will see a similar \nset up to the current event, with a slow-moving, strung out \nfront to our north bringing several chances for rain and storms \nfor the end of the week into next weekend.",
